dent to some vertex v\nof f. In this case the automorphism group Aut(M) of M acts transitively\n5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Stability of Rose Window graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/371290647_Stability_of_Rose_Window_graphs)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Wilson described nine families of unstable Rose Window graphs and conjectured that these contain all non-trivially unstable Rose Window graphs (2008). ... The aim of this paper is to ﬁll in this gap and show that the conjecture for the Rose ... The Rose Window graph R n(a, r) is deﬁned to have the vertex set {u i, v i:i∈Z n}, and\n\nTable 1. The nine families of unstable Rose Window graphs R n(a, r).\n\n•the Rose Window graphs,\n\n•the toroidal graphs.\n\nVarious families of unstable graphs were constructed for each class and Wilson con-\n\njectured that these families cover all non-trivially unstable graphs in the given class.\n\nThe conjecture has been conﬁrmed for the generalized Petersen graphs [19] and for the\n\ntoroidal graphs [24], whereas it has been disproved for the class of circulant graphs [18].\n\nTo the best of our knowledge, a characterization of the unstable circulant graphs of order\n\nn and valency k is known only in special cases: n is odd [5, 18], n= 2 p for a prime p[9]\n\nand k≤7 [10]. Up to now, no progress has been made on the Rose Window graphs.\n\nThe aim of this paper is to ﬁll in this gap and show that the conjecture for the Rose\n\nWindow graphs is also true.\n\nDeﬁnition 1.1 ([21]).Let n be an integer such that n≥3 and let a, r ∈Z n,a, r 6= 0.\n\nThe Rose Window graph R n(a, r) is deﬁned to have the vertex set {u i, v i:i∈Z n}, and\n\nedges\n\n{u i, u i+1},{v i, v i+r},{u i, v i},{u i+a, v i}, i ∈Z n,\n\nwhere the additions in the subscripts are done in Z n.\n\nHere and in what follows, Z n denotes the ring of residue classes of integers modulo n.\n\n-----